description = A space game across a whole galaxy: four million stars, each one there to visit, with twelve great powers in two clusters, their wars, small factions and currencies. Fly your ship with Newtonian flight and supercruise, jump between stars along routes or the powers' lanes, and dock at their ports to trade, take war, survey, delivery and ground survey contracts, refit in four module categories, and change ships among forty designs. Worlds turn and orbit in real time, at a twelfth of real size with real gravity. Land on any airless world on your vertical thrusters, among its hills and craters, with spotlights and night vision for the dark side; scan the ground for deposits, and sell first landings to cartographers. Surface ports stand on worlds too (starports, settlements, outposts, mining camps, heavy landing fields and forward bases with turrets): ask the traffic controller for a pad and wait your turn behind NPC traffic, or let an Autopilot Computer fly you in. Pirates raid them now and then: drive the raiders off for the holder's thanks, or the port is looted. Every port keeps its own market, with stock and demand that drift over the calendar; store ships and modules at ports and send for them. Insure your ship with a power and wake at its nearest port if you lose it. Rack up too big a bounty and the Interstellar Marshals come for you. Fight pirates and navies with lasers, gatlings, charged mass drivers, missiles and torpedoes; mine asteroid rings and belts; chart wild systems, find the ruins of older peoples and their blueprints; claim systems and build colonies, in orbit or on the ground, with surface mines, forward bases and the wars to hold them. At the galaxy's heart the Maw leads to the Rift, the galaxy upside down (two million stars and about 1,600 scattered havens), where the Riftborn hunt and the Precursors' ruins hold the strongest ships and modules there are. Pilots: six save slots sharing one galaxy, each with its own difficulty (Rookie, Pilot or Admiral) and, if you dare, Hardcore: higher rewards and a Contraband Merchant at major starports, whose exclusive variants are bound to the pilot who buys them, but one life: dying wipes the pilot, though the galaxy keeps what they did. Co-op: fly as a wing of up to four in the host's galaxy, on your local network (Rudolf OS's duel service) or online once the relay is up. Dock and trade at the host's ports, take on the missions the host shares, or split up and roam on your own; each pilot is saved at home. Duels: four ships built for it, an asteroid field, best of three rounds, against an AI pilot or another pilot over a network. Simple or Full controls, a radial menu, Combat and Analysis modes; a settings menu for graphics, sound and the HUD, rebindable keys and gamepad buttons, nine languages (following Rudolf OS's by default) and accessibility options. Keyboard and mouse, a controller, or touch with an on-screen stick for phones. Still to come: the Core's story, and the Precursor cloaking device it awards a Hardcore pilot. Made for Rudolf OS.
